WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.880
ROBERT-JAN HUIJSMAN:
In this talk

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.530
I would like to talk to
you first about Firebase

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.110
and what Firebase is,
what it can do for you

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.010
what it can do for your apps.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.260
I'll show you a few examples.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.060
I'll talk you through
the whole thing.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.390
But then I will also introduce
you to some of my friends

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.210
here from NU.NL, NUsport.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.090
They have built an actual
app on all the stuff

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.830
that I will be
talking to you about,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.310
and they're excited to
tell you about their app.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.520
But first, let me
tell you a little bit

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.450
about what a Firebase is.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.160
Firebase is basically your
front door to Google's Cloud

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.970
if you build apps, and apps, in
the broadest sense of the word,

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.000
be it mobile apps or a web app.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.520
If you are building
something with a small team,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.900
if you're building something
with mobile users or web users,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.760
you can use Firebase as your
front door to Google's Cloud.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.410
What would that bring you?

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.990
Well, Firebase has
a suite of products

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.270
that help you with
app development,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.550
and it works on all platforms.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.150
It works on iOS and
Android, clearly--

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.450
these are the dominant mobile
platforms-- as well as the web,

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.450
as well as for game developers,
C++, Unity, the whole shebang.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.200
So on all of those
platforms, we can give you

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.720
a number of tools that will
help you develop your app.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.710
And I'll talk through a
number of the highlights,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.260
but the key point of this slide
is quite how many there are.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.430
There turn out to be
many issues that you need

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.340
to solve when you build an app.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.380
And we hope to attack at least
all of the hard ones for you

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.590
so that you can spend more
time doing the fun code,

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.920
and we do the
nasty part for you.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.070
So let's dive into a
few of the features

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.972
that help you develop your app.

00:02:03.972 --> 00:02:05.430
We have a few
features as well that

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.470
help you grow your
usage, et cetera,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:08.940
once you have a successful app.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.980
But let's first dive into
how you develop an app.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.800
The first feature, and the one
that many, many people start

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.870
with when they start
to use Firebase,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.240
is our realtime database.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.520
And this is-- it's
a NoSQL database.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.210
It's a database that can store--

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.240
imagine it as an
object in the cloud,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.910
and your applications
can connect

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.880
to parts of that object,
listen to changes.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.980
It does a whole bunch
of stuff for you,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.670
for example, the
synchronization between devices.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.280
Let's say that you
have a bunch of devices

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.980
that are all interested in
the same set of your data.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.660
For example, I, right now, am
carrying two phones, as well as

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.910
a laptop and a tablet,
in my bag over there.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.370
I might have them
with me all the time.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.530
If I press a button
on my phone, I

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.280
would like to see the
change on my laptop as well.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.110
The realtime database
does all of that for you.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.210
This is the realtime--

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.070
the realtime part
of it is that when

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.050
I press that button on
my phone, it immediately

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.080
changes on all of my
other devices as well.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.890
It takes care of that
synchronization for me.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.470
The other part that
is cool is that it

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:24.550
does reconciliation of things
like poor connectivity.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.420
So let's say that my tablet
that is making a change

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.170
is currently offline because
the Wi-Fi here is oversubscribed

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.380
with thousands of people,
and it doesn't currently

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.340
have a connection.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.460
It'll buffer those updates,
and when my tablet next

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.110
comes online again, it'll
sync all of those changes

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.030
to all my other devices.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.220
And I didn't have to
write a line of code

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.610
to make that happen.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:53.370
So compare this to a model where
you would write your own API.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.140
You would have to
deal with the fact

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.760
that your tablet was offline.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.260
You would have to
deal with the fact

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.843
that maybe other
changes have happened

00:03:59.843 --> 00:04:02.080
in the meantime, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.250
All of that done for you.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.950
Authentication is
the next feature

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.050
that I think many
people look at.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:14.610
Authentication does user
sign in, either with username

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.850
and password or with a
phone, with a phone number,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.630
with SMS verification, or with
an OAuth provider, something

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.050
like Google or Facebook, or
your own existing auth system

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:28.350
if you already have it, as well
as anonymous, and a whole bunch

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.560
more.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.567
So this is a problem that
every app developer has,

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:33.150
approximately.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.220
You need to sign in your users.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.930
You need to know who they are.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.080
Firebase Auth does it for
you with an out-of-the-box UI

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.770
in about two lines of code.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:45.900
So the next time you're
writing a log in page, don't.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.020
Hosting will let
you host a website.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.240
It'll give you a
free SSL certificate.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.630
It'll connect to
your existing domain.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:59.500
We have storage which lets you
put large files in the cloud.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.070
And there's a whole bunch
more of these features that

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.640
help you do things that
many app developers need

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:10.260
and many app developers have
had to reinvent in the past.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.880
No more.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.480
Now, let's say that you have an
existing app, and you like it,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.950
and your users like it.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.920
And they use it, but you'd
like there to be more users,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.939
or you'd like your users
to be more engaged.

00:05:22.939 --> 00:05:24.480
Hopefully, this is
a problem you will

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.440
get to once you have
an app that works.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.160
And so we have a
number of features

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.510
that help you grow your app.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.150
Firebase Cloud
Messaging helps you

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:37.080
do things like send
messages to your users.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.090
Things like push
notifications, et cetera,

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.720
are something that many of us
have had to build in the past.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.870
Firebase Cloud Messaging
helps you do that.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:49.470
Remote configuration helps
you do things like hmm,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:55.050
I have built a game, and level
3 is a little bit too hard,

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.930
and I'm losing too many
gamers at that level.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.780
Let me tweak down
the difficulty level

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.600
of that level a little bit.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.450
Done.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.890
And what Remote
Configuration does

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.140
is it synchronizes that,
basically, parameter

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:12.300
change to all of your users
to, to the million users that

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.070
have installed your
app, without them

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.160
having to update their app.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.830
Great.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.180
Analytics.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.480
If any of you have ever used
Google Analytics on the web,

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.640
you have thought
to yourself, wow,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.380
what would I do without it?

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.690
Because it tells you
so much about what

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.760
your users are doing
on the website.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.830
It tells you so much about
where they came from.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.100
Now you can all of that
in your app as well.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.900
And I do promise you, if you
have an app that uses this,

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.780
your next app will also use
it because you will never

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.171
want to do without it again.

00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:47.920
And the nice thing
about analytics

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.380
is that it is a free product.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.630
By the way, all of the
products I talk about

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:55.240
are free to use for, like,
hobby sort of things.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.130
You can experiment with
it today if you want,

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.410
no credit card needed, even.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:01.180
Analytics is free forever.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.580
So you can use it for--

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:06.280
I don't know-- if you're
NU.NL, for example.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.810
So we have many,
many cool features

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.100
that help you build apps.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.860
This is just a few of them.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.860
But how do you put
them all together?

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:21.470
How do you meld them
all into one app

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:26.630
and respond to what happens in
all of these different systems

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:28.380
as things happen?

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.910
The answer to that--

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.315
oh, sorry.

00:07:32.315 --> 00:07:35.910
I can give a few examples
where this is a problem.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:39.280
For example, if I were using
a traditional backend, not

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.770
Firebase, all of these
things would be hard.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:44.390
For example, let's
say that I want

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.340
to send a notification
to my users

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.450
when they have received
a new chat message.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.910
I need to be running a backend.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:55.760
That backend has to be able
to receive messages from users

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.170
all across the globe.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.570
It has to be able to persist
those messages somewhere,

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.490
and then have some
sort of integration

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.860
with, for example,
Firebase Cloud Messaging,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:09.330
to send out the message to the
user who received the message.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.960
Or another example,
resizing images

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:17.040
once a user uploads a photo
to Firebase Cloud Storage.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.380
Let's take that example and
dive into it a little bit more.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:25.850
So I have an app, and it wants
to upload a picture to storage

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.650
and resize it.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.180
In the past, what
you would do is

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.580
you would talk to
your custom backend,

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.809
and it would say, aha,
let me place the original

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:40.610
in one place on storage, and
then let me resize the image

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.030
and put it in a different
place on storage.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.191
Ta-da, I'm done.

00:08:44.191 --> 00:08:44.690
Great.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.700
This seems like it would work.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.460
And it seems effortless, right?

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.130
Until you realize
that, yes, this

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.380
gives you the
thumbnail, the resizing.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.330
It does not give you
resumable uploads

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:57.480
of your original picture.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.170
It does not give you resumable
downloads of either the result

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.740
or the original picture.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.769
It does not give you
end user authentication,

00:09:03.769 --> 00:09:05.810
whether they were permitted
to write that picture

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:06.890
in the first place.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.690
It does not give
you a pay-as-you-go,

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:12.170
sort of cloudy model
that we've come to love,

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:15.800
because you have to run that
physical virtual machine

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.400
or even a piece of
actual hardware.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.800
So it's not a
pay-as-you-go model.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.750
And it certainly
isn't global scale,

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:26.270
unless you do a lot of work to
do this all across the globe.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.860
And then, again, you
will pay even more.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:36.620
So using Firebase instead
of that traditional backend,

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.530
all of these things
become a whole lot easier

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.540
thanks to Cloud Functions.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.860
This is the newest item
in our lineup, the newest

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:50.540
product in our develop
tier of products.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:52.650
And it makes all of
these things easier.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:56.480
So let's take another
look at the example

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.200
of the photo being uploaded.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.750
The way Functions
works is something

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.950
happens, and you'd like to
do something in response.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.350
So for example, a photo is
uploaded to Cloud Storage,

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.110
and I would like to
create the thumbnail.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.980
Using Cloud Functions,
that is really easy,

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.270
because this photo uploaded
to Cloud Storage is an event.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:21.890
You receive that event.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.440
As a Cloud Function,
you trigger off that.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.950
You do something, for example,
creating the thumbnail.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.942
To give a little bit more of a
complicated example, let's dive

00:10:31.942 --> 00:10:33.650
into the realm of
machine learning, which

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:36.950
I know lots of you
have heard about today,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:41.000
before, things like I
upload a sound file.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.660
I might say, [DUTCH].

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:48.230
The Cloud Speech API
understands that this

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.710
is Dutch that I just spoke,
transcribes it, and puts that

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.880
into a database.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.280
So we used a Cloud Function to
realize that a sound file had

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:04.910
been uploaded to the cloud, to
push that to the Cloud Speech

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:06.530
API, which does
machine learning,

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:11.720
to recognize that speech, and
we wrote it back to Firebase.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:14.570
But now, now that I've
written this back to Firebase,

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.244
Firebase has an event,
which is something

00:11:17.244 --> 00:11:18.410
was written to the database.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.300
And we can trigger
off of that again.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:23.570
And we can say, OK, let me
translate the word [DUTCH]

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:26.660
into English, for example,
and get the result "cat,"

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.780
and write that back
into the database.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:33.460
So we built a pipeline,
a data pipeline of sorts,

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.180
with machine learning, et
cetera, built in, with--

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:41.420
well, this turns out to be
about 10ish lines of code.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:45.130
The example that I gave
here actually exists.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:49.650
You can go to saythat.io/source,
and you can see on GitHub

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.340
the code that we've
written for that.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.650
And you can clone
it, play with it.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:55.840
It works.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:00.330
So you're wondering
what sort of events

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:03.120
can I trigger Cloud
Functions off of?

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.980
Well, first and foremost,
we have HTTP endpoints.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:11.520
This is a pretty standard, I'd
say, model that we're used to.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.100
Somebody comes and hits
your website, basically.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.280
You want to respond to that.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:16.590
This generates dynamic content.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:20.220
It's great for generating
dynamic websites.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.050
But since this is in
the Firebase world,

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.610
we also work with the
Firebase realtime database.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.390
We work with Cloud Pub/Sub,
with authentication--

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.600
this is the user sign in system
that I just talked about.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:35.970
So for example, a
new user signs up.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.320
You would like to hear about it.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.880
Great.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.140
Cloud storage for Firebase,
as well as Google Analytics

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.330
for Firebase-- this
is the tool that

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.380
tells you what your users
are doing in the app.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:50.850
So for example, you might
get something like a "Oh, my.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.130
Oh, dear, there was a crash."

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.300
And this user is one of the
most valuable users that I have.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.660
Let me send them a coupon
code just to say sorry.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.640
Great, that engages your user.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.190
It keeps the high
value user in your app,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:08.280
even though they've just
had a bad experience.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:11.940
Firebase Hosting lets
you host your web pages.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.400
Now those web pages can
be dynamically generated

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.140
using Functions.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:20.550
And we have more of
these coming up soon.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:24.540
So here's an example of
some actual code for a Cloud

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.226
Function.

00:13:26.226 --> 00:13:29.400
And I should pause
for a moment and say,

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.920
you've probably heard
about Cloud Functions

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.190
before me just standing here
from other people standing

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:35.850
here.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:41.040
Cloud Functions exists in the
wider cloud world as well.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.980
In the Firebase world,
the app development world,

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.140
we've added a few tricks to
make things easier for you.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:52.200
So for example, in this
case, we're using Node.js.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.750
And this Node.js imports
an SDK that we've

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:58.200
written for you to make
writing functions as easy as we

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.820
could make it.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:04.230
So our top line says,
I will import this SDK

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:05.580
that we've written.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:07.712
And then we export a function.

00:14:07.712 --> 00:14:09.420
And we are going to
call it Hello world--

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:14.050
gee, how creative--
and we say functions.

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:16.740
This is an HTTP function.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.520
And I would like you to
respond to a request.

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.740
You get the request
and the response

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.720
object when the actual
request happens.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.050
This is much like a
Node.js express app,

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.150
if you've ever used
any of these before.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:33.280
And you can send the
result that you would like.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.100
Now the cool thing
about this is to get

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:36.750
this running in
the cloud, all you

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:40.320
do is type on your command
line, Firebase Deploy.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:46.500
If I go back one step,
you see that in this line,

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.630
we had all of the
information we needed

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:52.090
to create a function that
actually runs in the cloud.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:57.420
And what we do when you
run Firebase Deploy here,

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.910
we read your code
for you, we see

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.190
what functions you would
like to run in the cloud,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:03.240
we run them for you.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:04.020
It is that easy.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.030
There's no flags.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.030
It is, for me at least,
the simplest solution

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.340
that I know of running code
in a globally scalable way.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.512
Here's a slightly more
complicated example.

00:15:17.512 --> 00:15:18.970
The thing I wanted
to call out here

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.650
is that the system
works the same

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:28.150
when you have not an HTTPS
endpoint, but a database event

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.470
that you want to listen to.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:32.440
So for example, you
say Functions Database

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:35.740
when there is a write to
the following reference.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:40.037
On that write, give me an event
and trigger a bunch of code.

00:15:40.037 --> 00:15:41.620
And this particular
code is an example

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:44.410
where we take the time
stamp of the write,

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:46.690
and we write it to the
database so that we can always

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:49.990
persist a time stamp with
every write that happens.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:51.550
So this is the old
world that we were

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:55.000
in before we were
using Firebase, where

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.580
our mobile apps had
to talk to an API that

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:00.010
had to talk to a backend,
and the backend, maybe,

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:02.710
had some custom code
running in it, et cetera.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:05.080
In the new world, you
take your custom code,

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:08.320
you upload it to the
cloud, to Firebase,

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.900
and it glues the whole
application together.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:16.120
So by using the various
Firebase features,

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.820
we address all the
various pain points

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:21.280
you get during app
development, and we just

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:26.620
get from zero to app much,
much faster than we did before.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.280
And by using Functions, all
of those different features

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:34.200
now feel like one platform.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:38.390
So that's my brief
introduction to what Firebase

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.700
is, what you might use it for,
and how you use Functions,

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.320
which we're all
very excited about.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:48.900
But you've probably heard a lot
of Googlers talk about, yeah,

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.350
I'm so excited about the thing
that I work on, which is true.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.540
But for you, I
think it's much more

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.990
interesting to hear about an
actual use case, something

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.770
from the real world, people
who've actually used Firebase

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:03.720
to great effect.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:07.290
So I would like to invite
up my friends from NUsport,

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.839
and they're going to tell
you about a real app using

00:17:09.839 --> 00:17:10.832
Firebase.

00:17:10.832 --> 00:17:14.213
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:20.740
ISTVAN FONAY: Yes, thanks
for a cool presentation, RJ.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.240
So we are from NUsport.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.640
As you might know,
that's part of NU.NL.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.540
And we would like
to show you today

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.985
how we used Firebase to
create the fastest sport

00:17:28.985 --> 00:17:30.340
app of the Netherlands.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:32.640
My name is Istvan, and
my colleague is Johnny.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.980
He's one of our awesome
backend developers at NU.NL.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:40.630
So a brief introduction
about NUsport.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:43.390
NUsport is sport at its best.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:46.870
And what we aim to do is to
provide realtime, instantaneous

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:49.580
news to our users about
their favorite sport events,

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:52.130
even if there are more
of them at the same time.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:54.280
So some facts and figures.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:57.280
NUsport has around
300,000 downloads,

00:17:57.280 --> 00:17:59.950
1.3 million weekly page views.

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:02.560
And we are also quite happy
that our users are engaged.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:05.640
So during matches we
have, for example, polls.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:09.380
And we have usually
around 10,000 poll votes

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:12.520
on these votes,
which is really nice.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:16.150
And we also have
1,500 blogs weekly

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:18.740
in the app, which might
sound weird at the moment.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:21.550
But as soon as you will see
how the NUsport app looks,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.890
then you will see
what I mean by that.

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:27.730
So a bit spoiled this already,
but the goal of the NUsport

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:31.360
app is to be the fastest
sport app of the Netherlands,

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:32.530
in short.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.300
And there's an
interesting thing I

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:37.630
would like to mention about the
development of the NUsport app.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:42.040
So we started to create the
new NUsport app last year,

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:43.240
before the Champions League.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.640
So we wanted to be done
for the Champions League.

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:48.280
And I don't know how is it
usually at your organization,

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:50.320
but at ours, it can
happen sometimes

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.690
that we have quite
challenging deadlines.

00:18:52.690 --> 00:18:54.790
And it was also the
case with NUsport.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:57.749
So we had two months to
develop two apps from scratch,

00:18:57.749 --> 00:19:00.040
and also create the real time
backend, and the clients,

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:01.910
and everything we needed for it.

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:06.091
So we had a few
options to do that.

00:19:06.091 --> 00:19:07.840
One of the options was
to go to the market

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.690
and buy a complete third party
package, software package,

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:15.560
with the realtime backend,
with clients, everything.

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:17.200
But that cost quite
some money, and we

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:18.866
like to spare money
where it's possible.

00:19:18.866 --> 00:19:20.720
So that was not an option.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:23.650
The other option was to
create the realtime backend

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:25.110
and the clients ourself.

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:27.610
But back then, we didn't have
too much experience with that.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:29.170
And due to the
deadline, it would

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:31.753
have been quite challenging with
experimenting and everything,

00:19:31.753 --> 00:19:33.350
so we also didn't
want to do that.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.610
So our best option was
to choose Firebase.

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:37.960
So we did that.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:40.600
And there are also some other
reasons why we chose Firebase.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.000
So I wanted to
mention three here.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:45.500
It's fast, it scales with
you, and it's cost efficient.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.530
So it's not only
fast in the sense

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.540
that-- you will
soon see in the demo

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:52.150
when we push
something to the app,

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:55.540
it shows instantaneously
in realtime.

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:58.150
But it's also fast in
the sense that it's

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:01.210
really easy to implement for
iOS and for Android as well.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:03.504
And you can implement it
really easily and fast.

00:20:03.504 --> 00:20:05.920
It also scales with you, which
is really important for us.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:09.000
Because as you might imagine,
the nature of the NUsport app

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:09.980
is a bit cyclical.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:11.840
So for example,
on a busy Sunday,

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.800
when the [INAUDIBLE] stop
is rocking it in Formula 1,

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.140
we have an IX and Feyenoord
game, and maybe even

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:20.350
cycling event going on, then
we have much more users than

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.390
on a boring Tuesday
morning at 9:00

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:24.040
AM when there's
nothing happening.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.860
So it's very important for
us that yeah, our systems

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.510
can scale with us.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:31.600
Firebase just does that.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:33.140
And that leads to my next month.

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:35.440
It's also quite cost efficient.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:38.780
Because as Robert-Jan mentioned,
it's a pay-as-you-go model.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:40.990
So obviously, on
Sundays when we have

00:20:40.990 --> 00:20:44.800
more users and higher loads, and
you use more data, we pay more.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.130
But on Tuesdays, when it's
boring, then we pay less.

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:53.080
And that also keeps our
managers happy [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:57.000
So how are we actually
using Firebase?

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:59.320
I would like to mention
three features here

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:00.800
which are interesting.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:02.932
The realtime database.

00:21:02.932 --> 00:21:04.390
So if you look at
the app, then you

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:07.120
see that we have some blogs.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:10.700
And this is actually a timeline.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:12.792
And as the new sport
events come in,

00:21:12.792 --> 00:21:14.500
then they always end
up on the top right.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:17.110
So it's a bit like a
Twitterish timeline.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:21.760
And we use different blogs
to show these newsbeats

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:22.570
to our users.

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:26.530
So we have pictures, videos,
Instagram, Twitter blogs,

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:27.830
all type of things.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:30.220
And this is all done with
the help of Firebase.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:31.870
We also have ball blogs.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.640
And also, we have
the scoreboard,

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:35.590
which you can see
in the top there,

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:37.720
which obviously, you
have to update nonstop

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:39.340
because the match
is going on, so you

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.980
need to show the minutes
of the game and the goals.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.214
And that's done in
realtime, and the user

00:21:44.214 --> 00:21:45.380
doesn't need to do anything.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.410
So he doesn't need to swipe
down or press something,

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:49.120
but it's just being
pushed to his device.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:52.070
And the end user, obviously,
has the newest information.

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:54.220
So that's pretty cool.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:56.140
We also use Cloud Messaging.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:58.910
So obviously, we use
push notifications.

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:00.730
And we use it to
personalize the experience

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:01.820
of the users in the app.

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:04.390
So users can sign up
for different sports,

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:07.900
like football,
Formula 1 cycling,

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:09.910
and they get the most
important news delivered

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:12.220
in the form of push messages.

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:14.650
Another interesting
thing is Remote Config.

00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:18.480
We use this to optimize a bit
the user experience in the app.

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.050
So when you open the
app, we can set--

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.850
remotely, on the
fly, without needing

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:26.030
to submit a new app to the
app store, or whatever--

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.470
we can set how many blogs
appear for you on the screen.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:33.720
So in this way, we can optimize
the loading speed of the app.

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:35.860
So these are things
that we are doing now.

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:37.850
But we also have some
additional plans.

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:40.820
The first plan to implement
Firebase for NU.NL.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:43.130
And this we already
achieved, actually.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:47.150
So if you can imagine, NU.NL
app is a totally different scale

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.340
than a NUsport app, so
NU.NL is the biggest news

00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:50.590
site of the Netherlands.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:53.450
We have millions and millions
of users every month.

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:57.710
So then we use Firebase
for breaking news events.

00:22:57.710 --> 00:22:59.900
So when something really
big happens, and you

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.300
come to our website or apps--

00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:05.450
so we use it on iOS,
Android, and web.

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:07.010
When you come to
the website, then

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:10.042
if you click on the article
about the breaking news event,

00:23:10.042 --> 00:23:11.750
then you will see the
same blog structure

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:13.890
as you can see in
the NUsport app.

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:16.820
So we use Firebase there as
well, and it works really well.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:20.770
And it also scales really good
in doing these breaking news

00:23:20.770 --> 00:23:22.520
events, when we have
hundreds of thousands

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:27.230
of people coming to
our apps and website.

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:28.521
Firebase Analytics.

00:23:28.521 --> 00:23:30.770
You can do really cool things
with Firebase Analytics,

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:32.978
and also in combination with
other Firebase products.

00:23:32.978 --> 00:23:35.050
So for example, with
Cloud Messaging--

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:36.520
in the NUsport app,
for example, we

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:40.750
could create user groups,
user segments on the fly.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:43.190
So if we see that people
are clicking on the IX

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:46.880
and Feyenoord blogs, then
we can create a segment,

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:48.260
or send a push
notification, only

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:51.590
to those people who click on
these blogs with something

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:53.990
personal, and with a
promotion, or whatsoever.

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:56.020
So you can do really
cool things with that.

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:58.220
And Cloud Functions.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:01.477
Back then, when we started
to develop the NUsport App,

00:24:01.477 --> 00:24:03.060
Cloud Functions was
not available yet.

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:05.420
So we didn't use
it at the start.

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:07.190
But now since it's
available, we see

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:10.610
some really cool opportunities
how we can implement it.

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:12.560
And that's something
what Johnny will tell you

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:14.625
more about with the demo.

00:24:14.625 --> 00:24:15.500
JOHNNY MIJNHOUT: Yes.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:16.300
Thank you, Istvan.

