Hi, everybody.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:21.470
I'll give you a small
demo of the NUsport app.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:23.230
If you can just
unlock the phone.

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:23.980
ISTVAN FONAY: Yes.

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:25.105
JOHNNY MIJNHOUT: Thank you.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:32.240
Yes, it works.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:34.630
So you can see our
content management system

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:36.040
in the first column.

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:38.710
This is where we add the blogs.

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:42.010
We have several types of blogs.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:45.260
I will demo the text
blog, but as you can see,

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:49.210
if I click here, it has a
dropdown list with some types.

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.230
We integrate with
Facebook, Instagram.

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:55.240
We can add balls, et cetera.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.900
The middle column is
the Firebase console,

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:00.820
where you can see
our database, which

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.460
is a pretty simple structure.

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.910
But before you
get through there,

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:07.420
when you're used to using
relational database,

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.280
that's kind of tricky.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.820
But we managed it,
and it's working.

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:16.210
And the last column
is the actual app.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:21.160
And you can see a scoreboard
blog, and an image blog,

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:25.440
and at the bottom
there is a text blog.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:29.080
And you can see them also here
inside our content management

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.830
system.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:35.230
And I will add a new
text blog, something

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:41.360
like "I am Johnny at NUsport.

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:45.040
Color is red.

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.680
Catch me outside.

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:50.290
How about that?"

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:53.560
So I store it, and
you'll see it's

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:57.430
added to the Firebase
database and instantly

00:25:57.430 --> 00:26:00.140
being added inside the app.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:02.610
And what's cool is we don't
have the load of the user.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:05.880
So if we have one user, or
if we have a million users,

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:09.710
everyone connects to
the Firebase database.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:13.480
So if a very simple machine
running our upend server,

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:19.240
and we don't have to scale
up when more users are added.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:23.530
So I can, of course,
also edit the blog.

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:32.245
Just a typing mistake
I can see here.

00:26:32.245 --> 00:26:34.590
And some here.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:37.650
So when I store it, you'll
see an update coming here,

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:42.210
and also the app is also
immediately updated.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:46.500
Maybe you also noticed
the scoreboard blog--

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:47.990
I'm back to here--

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:50.310
is still sitting on top.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:52.590
But I added a new blog,
and it appeared below it.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:57.440
And we added some functionality
we call painting blogs.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.250
As you can see
here, the scoreboard

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:00.990
has a tiny pane added.

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:04.500
And when you add blogs
or content to Firebase,

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.770
you can also add
a hidden priority

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:10.540
field, which is just a number.

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:13.596
And it's ordered from 0 to--

00:27:13.596 --> 00:27:18.510
yeah, up, running,
so it's 0 to 9.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:20.040
And we created something.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:26.160
The not been blogs
are just a time stamp

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.560
of when they are created,
but are negative.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:30.720
So we have new ones on top.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.180
And the pin blogs are
hard numbers, which

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:34.810
is a very low negative number.

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:38.420
So we can pin them, always on
top, which about 100 years.

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.990
From then, I won't be here,
so nobody will blame me.

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:46.240
So that's about it, I think.

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.540
I can also delete
it, and you can

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.340
see it deleting from Firebase.

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:52.320
And leaving from here.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:53.750
And it's always this fast.

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:56.160
So it doesn't matter
if 1 million users are

00:27:56.160 --> 00:28:01.500
connected to it, or one user,
it just works instantly.

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.360
So back to the presentation.

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:12.810
And these are some learnings
we had during development

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:14.850
and when we were done.

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:18.440
We're never done,
but somewhat done.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:19.980
One was the bandwidth usage.

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:22.400
You are charged for the
bandwidth you are using.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:25.770
We have a pay-as-you-go--

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:26.520
how do you say it?

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:27.290
ISTVAN FONAY: Model.

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:28.331
JOHNNY MIJNHOUT: --model.

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:36.890
And it turned out that we were
using about 1 terabyte of data

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:38.390
per month.

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.670
And there was
something in our data

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.390
structure in the beginning.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:46.332
We were looking to
too much updates.

00:28:46.332 --> 00:28:48.290
So if you don't subscribe
to the right endpoint

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:49.706
inside your database,
then you get

00:28:49.706 --> 00:28:53.820
a lot of updates where
users are not watching.

00:28:53.820 --> 00:28:58.815
So you need to think before
you leap into this structure.

00:28:58.815 --> 00:29:01.494
And it's also, at one
point, a NoSQL database,

00:29:01.494 --> 00:29:02.660
which you already mentioned.

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:05.690
We're used to using
relational databases.

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:09.500
And now we had to flatten our
structure and keep all simple.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.330
So that's something, yeah.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:16.375
It's not very hard, but you need
to think about it beforehand.

00:29:16.375 --> 00:29:20.330
And the final thing
is the sharding.

00:29:20.330 --> 00:29:22.460
As each one said,
Firebase scales with you,

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:24.890
but it scales to
a certain level.

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:27.980
And we didn't know before,
but there is a level.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:32.870
And there's a maximum of
100,000 concurrent users

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:36.260
per Firebase project/database.

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:38.580
And since we're also
using this for NU.NL

00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:43.280
when a news event happens,
it's pushed to all our clients,

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:46.320
and we have about more
than a million clients.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:49.320
So if they all open the
app at the same time,

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.500
they will go to
the same timeline.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.860
And then we will have
more than 100,000 users.

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:57.320
So we talked to Google
on how to fix that,

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:03.080
and they said, well, most users
are using sharding for this.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.060
And what it is--

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:09.630
we just have our main database,
and we just clone it 20 times.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.020
So we create 20
Firebase databases

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:14.030
which all contain the same data.

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:16.010
And we're updating
it from our backend,

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:17.630
so every time we
update a blog, we

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:19.910
send it to all the 20 shards.

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:21.470
And the clients just
randomly connect

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:22.830
to one of those 20 shards.

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:27.710
And now we have 20 times
100,000 concurrent connection

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:29.310
possibilities.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.310
So I think we can
manage with that.

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:35.420
But we'll see.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:38.400
Yeah, I think that's it.

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:42.580
I will give the word back to RJ.

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:45.436
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:49.670
ROBERT-JAN HUIJSMAN:
Thank you guys so much.

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:54.810
It's always just exciting to
see people using our stuff,

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:59.670
but like NU.NL using our stuff
is especially exciting to me,

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:01.410
personally, coming
from the Netherlands.

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:02.420
Thank you guys so much.

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:02.700
ISTVAN FONAY: Thanks.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:04.533
ROBERT-JAN HUIJSMAN:
Thank you guys so much.

00:31:04.533 --> 00:31:06.630
If you would like
to talk to us later,

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:10.650
we'll be hanging around for
a bit, and we'd love to chat.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.530
Thank you, and enjoy
the rest of the evening.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:14.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:19.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

